{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775323487-iGgqXjoJ9gdQ9De13fjr","role":"assistant","created":1775323491,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The document mentions that the personal saving rate was 4.6% in both 1989 and 1990, and it was below the 6.7% long-term average. However, I need to find historical data going back to 1950 to identify when the peak was. Let me check if there's more detailed data in the JSON file.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_d7d9e665fe634d69b7b4d0ee","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport json\ndata = json.load(open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1991_09.json'))\nelements = data['document']['elements']\nfor el in elements:\n    if 'content' in el and el['content']:\n        content = str(el['content'])\n        if 'saving' in content.lower() and ('percent' in content.lower() or 'rate' in content.lower()):\n            print(content[:500])\n            print('---')\n\""}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_a1102801-a929-49a9-a5c1-934b417935bc","role":"user","created":1775323491,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_d7d9e665fe634d69b7b4d0ee","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<table><tr><th colspan=\"2\">ECONOMIC POLICY</th><th>Page</th></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Moderate Growth Projected for U.S. Economy</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><th colspan=\"2\">TAX POLICY</th><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Abstracts of Recent Taxation Studies</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><th colspan=\"2\">TREASURY ISSUES INDEX</th><td>9</td></tr><tr><th colspan=\"3\">FINANCIAL OPERATIONS</th></tr><tr><th colspan=\"3\">PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY</th></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Growth of real gross national product</td><td\n---\nConsumer spending has begun to improve as employment opportunities increased in May and the moderation of inflation has helped to restore real purchasing power. Purchases of services such as medical care, personal care, transportation, household operations, education, personal business, and recreation have held up well even during the recession. Retail sales of durable and nondurable goods, including new cars, have strengthened following the disappointing results of last winter. Future gains are\n---\nRecent economic statistics have signaled the beginning of a moderate cyclical expansion in the United States. However, economic policies also must consider longer term issues. The imbalance of inadequate national savings compared with the need for increased investment to improve productivity must be corrected. The financial system must be upgraded and strengthened to meet the evolving needs of a dynamic economy. Economic planning must emphasize long-term priorities. Structural adjustments are ne\n---\n\"Role of Saving in a Dynamic U.S. Economy, The.\" September 1990, pp. 3-6. A report on the declining U.S. savings rate and its negative impact on investment and productivity growth in the United States.\